<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cadmus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kadmos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cadmus was a legendary hero of Greek myth, a Phoenician prince and brother of Europa, who founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, whose citadel was called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a> after him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> credits him with bringing the alphabet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He married the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harmonia">Harmonia</a>, and in later life the couple went to rule the Illyrian Enchelei before being turned into serpents.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cadmus.jpg" alt="Cadmus" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Python · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kadmos_dragon_Louvre_N3157.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the usual telling Cadmus was the son of Agenor, ruler of a Phoenician kingdom, and his wife Telephassa, although some sources give other parents. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> carried off his sister Europa, Cadmus was sent by his father to find her and told not to return without her. Unsuccessful, or unwilling to go against Zeus, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>, the island sacred to the Great Gods, where he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harmonia">Harmonia</a> as his bride; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> the wedding was the first on earth to which the gods brought gifts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cadmus went to Delphi, where the oracle told him to follow a cow marked with a half moon and to build a town where she lay down exhausted. The cow led him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. He killed the dragon that guarded the Ismenian spring and, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>'s instruction, sowed its teeth. Armed men called the Spartoi sprang up and fought one another until five survived, who helped him build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmeia</a>, the citadel of Thebes, and became the ancestors of its noblest families.</p><p>The dragon had been sacred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>, and the family met with misfortunes. Cadmus abdicated in favour of his grandson Pentheus and went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harmonia">Harmonia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> to fight beside the Enchelii, and he founded the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Lychnidos</a> and Bouthoe. Troubled by his ill fortune, he said that if the gods so loved the life of a serpent he might wish it for himself, and he began to change; Harmonia asked to share his fate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> credited Cadmus with bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Modern scholarship almost unanimously agrees that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a> came from the Phoenician, but the earliest Greek inscriptions match Phoenician letter forms of the late 9th or 8th centuries BCE, far later than Herodotus supposed. Frederick Ahl suggested that the legend might preserve memories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, which has been found in abundance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p><p>Albert Schachter suggested that Cadmus was an invented hero named after the Theban acropolis and made Phoenician through the influence of eastern immigrants. Others have proposed that he was a Mycenaean, a Cretan or an Argive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cadmus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek legend made <b>Cadmus</b>, a Phoenician prince from Tyre, the founder of the citadel and the teacher of the Phoenician alphabet, and set at Thebes the stories of Semele and Dionysus, Amphion's seven-gated wall, Oedipus and the Seven against Thebes, and the birth of Heracles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus says that the Greeks called their letters phoinikeia, “Phoenician letters”, brought by the companions of <b>Cadmus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Myth said <b>Cadmus</b> and Harmonia came to rule them after leaving Thebes, and that their descendants ruled the Illyrians.</blockquote><a href="/state/enchelei">Enchelei</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek myth gave the citadel its name from <b>Cadmus</b>, its legendary Phoenician founder.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-thebes">Mycenaean Kingdom of Thebes</a></li></ul></section>
